1
00:00:00,464 --> 00:00:03,865
Suppose you ask, “How does our order
validator work?”

2
00:00:03,918 --> 00:00:07,480
The name sounds specific to us because
we have the repository open.

3
00:00:08,183 --> 00:00:09,862
Look at the request, though.

4
00:00:09,942 --> 00:00:13,274
It contains neither a function nor a
file reference.

5
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:16,738
The assistant might search if this
session can read the workspace.

6
00:00:17,388 --> 00:00:21,317
Before trusting the explanation, find
the source it actually used.

7
00:00:23,597 --> 00:00:25,197
Look at the return line.

8
00:00:25,277 --> 00:00:28,742
A model reads pieces of this text called
tokens.

9
00:00:28,822 --> 00:00:33,017
In this illustration, the space before
quantity travels with the word,

10
00:00:33,071 --> 00:00:34,903
and the operator is another piece.

11
00:00:35,556 --> 00:00:38,754
Tokenizers can split the same line
differently.

12
00:00:38,834 --> 00:00:42,833
That matters when you're estimating how
much source, test output,

13
00:00:42,886 --> 00:00:45,055
and conversation can fit in a request.

14
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:50,638
Picture the next request after a long
debugging chat.

15
00:00:50,718 --> 00:00:55,170
It can include instructions, parts of
the conversation, selected files,

16
00:00:55,196 --> 00:00:56,292
and tool results.

17
00:00:56,931 --> 00:01:00,583
The client chooses what to include
within a finite budget.

18
00:01:00,663 --> 00:01:05,068
If you need the current condition to be
available, attach the small source file.

19
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,624
Then check whether the answer points
back to that file.

20
00:01:10,544 --> 00:01:14,302
Open order quantity dot pie in the
training repository.

21
00:01:14,382 --> 00:01:18,564
Attach it in Copilot chat and ask what
happens for zero and one.

22
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,399
Read the return expression with me.

23
00:01:21,506 --> 00:01:24,358
Zero is equal to zero, so it passes.

24
00:01:24,411 --> 00:01:26,964
One is greater than zero, so it passes
too.

25
00:01:27,613 --> 00:01:30,572
The code explains the observed behavior.

26
00:01:30,625 --> 00:01:34,227
We still need the issue to decide
whether that behavior is correct.

27
00:01:36,529 --> 00:01:37,968
Add the issue text.

28
00:01:38,021 --> 00:01:41,220
The rule now says an order needs at
least one item.

29
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:44,045
Compare that with the answer we just
traced.

30
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:47,820
The code allows zero because the
comparison includes equality.

31
00:01:48,447 --> 00:01:51,352
The issue requires zero to be rejected.

32
00:01:51,432 --> 00:01:55,830
Ask the assistant to cite both sources
so you can see where each claim came

33
00:01:55,883 --> 00:01:56,197
from.

34
00:01:58,419 --> 00:02:02,417
Try the question twice in two fresh
chats on the same branch.

35
00:02:02,524 --> 00:02:05,056
First attach the issue and the function.

36
00:02:05,136 --> 00:02:08,236
For the second run, add an unrelated
release note.

37
00:02:08,916 --> 00:02:11,848
Read each answer and locate its
evidence.

38
00:02:11,901 --> 00:02:14,727
The release note does not explain this
boundary.

39
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:17,977
Keep the two sources that let a teammate
check the claim quickly.

40
00:02:20,218 --> 00:02:24,216
Now check the explanation against the
original training code.

41
00:02:24,296 --> 00:02:27,984
In an approved runner, call the function
for zero and negative one.

42
00:02:28,637 --> 00:02:32,422
The output shows true for zero and false
for negative one.

43
00:02:32,502 --> 00:02:35,167
That agrees with the comparison you
read.

44
00:02:35,247 --> 00:02:39,592
If the result differs in another run,
compare the checkout and confirm which

45
00:02:39,645 --> 00:02:41,616
version of the function you executed.
